-// cosmo 4.0.2's libc.a(dlmalloc.o), rebuilt from the same source with one
-// change in the nsync lock path below: the per-heap mutex is skipped until the
-// process has created a thread. Upstream takes it on every malloc and free
-// from the first call, which costs a single-threaded program two atomic
-// operations per allocation for nothing; cosmopolitan master gates the same
-// lock on its new __isthreaded flag (commit 5ddb5c2ad). 4.0.2 has no such
-// flag, so the gate reads _pthread_count, which clone() bumps before the new
-// thread can run and which starts at 1 for the main thread. The decision is
-// latched: once a thread has existed the lock stays on for the life of the
-// process, so a thread's exit never flips a heap back to unlocked while
-// another thread may still be inside it. Everything else, including the
-// .inc files pulled from the toolchain's include tree, is upstream verbatim.
